---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Resident Investigation Tracker — Pre-Launch Guide';
const description =
  'Prepare a spoiler-safe Grave Seasons resident tracker for Ashenridge. Record confirmed observations and mark schedules, clues, and suspects as unknown until tested.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Guide', url: '/guide/' },
  { name: 'Resident Investigation Tracker', url: '/resident-investigation-tracker/' },
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.yuto.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Pre-launch preparation</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">
      Grave Seasons Resident Investigation Tracker
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      Grave Seasons places a supernatural murder mystery inside Ashenridge, a town with more than
      30 residents. Before launch, there is no verified suspect list, daily schedule, or universal
      killer route. This tracker helps you organize observations after release without turning a
      screenshot, rumor, or one-run outcome into a confirmed fact.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-8">
      <img src={ASSETS.yuto.src} alt="Official Grave Seasons screenshot captioned as talking to Yuto in the grocery store" width={ASSETS.yuto.width} height={ASSETS.yuto.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">{ASSETS.yuto.credit}. The image identifies a conversation setting, not a confirmed schedule or clue.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-page mt-8">
      <h2>What the confirmed premise gives you</h2>
      <p>
        Grave Seasons is described as a narrative farming game set in Ashenridge, where a supernatural
        serial killer is active. The store description frames the goal as protecting potential victims
        and uncovering the murders over one year. It lists more than 30 residents, more than 70
        interiors, and seven major locations. It also describes a replayable killer selection, with
        different reasoning, targets, and kill styles across playthroughs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those facts are enough to design a note-taking system, but not enough to name a culprit or
        predict an event. A resident appearing in a screenshot is not evidence that they are a suspect.
        A romantic option is not automatically safe. A location count does not tell us where a clue or
        event will appear. Keep the tracker ready, then fill it from the playable game.
      </p>

      <h2>Make one record per observed resident</h2>
      <p>
        Create a separate line for each person you actually meet. Record the resident's displayed name
        exactly as shown, the date and time in your save, the location, and what happened. Avoid a
        vague note such as “seemed suspicious.” Write the visible action instead: a conversation,
        arrival, departure, item handoff, clue reaction, or other event the game makes clear.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Name:</strong> copy the in-game spelling; leave blank until the game identifies them.</li>
        <li><strong>When:</strong> record the in-game day, season, and clock time if visible.</li>
        <li><strong>Where:</strong> use the displayed location name and note whether it was indoors or outdoors.</li>
        <li><strong>Observation:</strong> describe only what you saw or what dialogue explicitly stated.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> note the clue or quest entry that supports an interpretation.</li>
        <li><strong>Confidence:</strong> label it confirmed, repeated, untested, or disproved in this save.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This format separates an event from your theory about it. If a resident leaves a location after
        a conversation, the event is “left the location after conversation.” The interpretation that
        they were avoiding the player belongs in a separate theory note until another observation
        supports it.
      </p>

      <h2>Track locations without assuming a schedule</h2>
      <p>
        Ashenridge has many interiors and several major areas. Group observations by the names the game
        uses rather than inventing a route map before you can inspect the town. Mark a place as “visited”
        only after entering it, and record whether the game showed a new clue, resident, or interaction
        there. This will make it easier to revisit a location after a season changes.
      </p>
      <p>
        The official material describes a 24-hour day-and-night cycle and says the world changes with
        the seasons. It does not publish each resident's timetable, shop hours, or whether every
        interior remains open all day. Treat a missed meeting as an observation to investigate, not
        proof of a specific schedule. Return at another time and record what changed.
      </p>

      <h2>Separate evidence from suspicion</h2>
      <p>
        A useful investigation tracker has two layers. The evidence layer records the clue and where it
        came from. The theory layer records what you think it might mean. This is especially important
        when the game can select different killers between runs: an outcome in one campaign may not
        describe the next campaign's identity, target, or reasoning.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Write down the exact clue wording or the visible event.</li>
        <li>Attach its in-game day, location, season, and any quest name.</li>
        <li>List which residents it directly mentions, if any.</li>
        <li>Keep interpretations in a separate column and mark them unconfirmed.</li>
        <li>After a replay, compare the same event rather than merging two runs together.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Use a run label for each campaign</h2>
      <p>
        Because each playthrough can vary, create a short campaign label before your first notes, such
        as “Run A — first save.” Do not use a guessed killer name as the label. Keep the date, major
        decisions, visible victim events, and quest progress under that run. If you begin again, create
        Run B rather than overwriting the earlier record.
      </p>
      <p>
        This also protects spoiler boundaries. If you share notes, label a confirmed late-game result
        clearly and leave unresolved residents out of a “killer” heading. A guide can then say “this
        run's evidence points to…” without claiming that the answer applies to every player.
      </p>

      <h2>Do not assume romance status answers the case</h2>
      <p>
        The game description lists 12 potential romanceable residents and says a few may also be the
        killer. That warning makes romance a reason to keep notes carefully, not a shortcut to a suspect
        answer. Record the resident, dialogue choice, date, and visible relationship result. Do not
        infer that a romance choice protects someone, makes them guilty, or changes the investigation
        unless the game shows that result.
      </p>

      <h2>A printable quick template</h2>
      <p>Use one row for every event worth checking later:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Run:</strong> A, B, or another label</li>
        <li><strong>Day and season:</strong> as displayed in the save</li>
        <li><strong>Time:</strong> exact clock time if visible; otherwise “unknown”</li>
        <li><strong>Resident:</strong> displayed name or “not identified”</li>
        <li><strong>Location:</strong> exact area or interior label</li>
        <li><strong>Event:</strong> factual description of what happened</li>
        <li><strong>Clue or quest:</strong> exact entry, or “none shown”</li>
        <li><strong>Interpretation:</strong> hypothesis, clearly marked unconfirmed</li>
        <li><strong>Retest:</strong> what you need to check on another day or run</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Details that remain unknown before launch</h2>
      <p>
        This is a preparation guide, not a solved route. The public game description does not provide
        the complete resident roster, exact schedules, a fixed culprit, clue order, suspect scoring,
        romance consequences, or a universal way to save each potential victim. It also does not say
        that every branch can be reversed or that every event can be repeated from a convenient save.
        Steam describes decisions as checked and autosaved, so record choices instead of assuming a
        manual undo is available.
      </p>
      <p>
        After release, update this page only when a resident, clue, or schedule is observed in the game.
        Include the run and version context for behavior that may vary. Until then, the safest advice is
        simple: keep names, events, clues, and theories in separate fields, and leave unknown details
        marked unknown.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
